60 minutes plus is looking at the  continuing fallout from a dark period in  our history for decades it was legal to  send someone to prison in the state of  louisiana even if the jury was not  unanimous these so-called jim crow  juries were created after the  emancipation of slaves to make it easier  to imprison people of color louisiana  voters overturned the law three years  ago but many people incarcerated under  those juries are still fighting for a  retrial  60 minutes plus correspondent wesley  lowry spoke with 52-year-old anthony  bolt who's been in prison for decades  there are 600 men and women still  incarcerated some of us serving life  sentences without probation  parole or suspension of sentence  predicated on a law that was devised to  reinstitute slavery in the state of  louisiana in 1994 bolt was charged with  second-degree murder and second-degree  attempted murder after a shooting at a  house party left one man dead and both  bolt and another man wounded  he insists he was not the shooter but  acknowledges he had a physical  altercation with the victim that night  the following year bolt was convicted  do you remember the day that the verdict  came in oh yes like yesterday what was  that day like for you it wasn't shocking  that i was convicted  it was kind of like a  numbness and when i discovered that i  was convicted by a non-unanimous jury it  went from a numbness to a shock really  the jurors had been split with 10 voting  to convict and two voting to acquit  in nearly every other state in the  country that tally would have resulted  in a hung jury and a mistrial  instead  bolt was sentenced to life in prison  so two of the jurors voted to acquit yes  what did you make of that the idea that  two jurors said this guy's not guilty  well at the time um  to be honest i was on the impression  that you had to be convicted by  unanimous jury so you didn't even  realize this could happen no not at the  time i was young and i had never been to  trial and  how did you feel when you realized that  i just couldn't believe that i was i was  going to be sentenced to spend the rest  of my life in prison  where  two jurors found me not guilty do you  consider that an injustice of course it  is of course it's an injustice and  wesley lowry joins us now wesley doesn't  feel right and voters in louisiana  actually decided it is not right they  overturned this law three years ago what  happened  so it's a little bit complicated as you  guys noted great these laws were put in  place following emancipation because  louisiana wanted to take steps they said  this explicitly in the state legislature  to retain a system of white supremacy  keep it easier to keep locking up black  men and black women this law stated in  the books for a century right up until  just a few years ago you could be  convicted  with just nine or ten votes for  conviction so you could have two or  three jurors who said this person is not  guilty  voters in louisiana overturn the law  but that what that did was it meant that  people couldn't be convicted on split  juries moving forward it didn't do very  much for someone like anthony bolt who  had already been convicted by a split  jury the supreme court would later come  in  and kind of uphold what the louisiana  voters said they they said look it's  clearly unconstitutional this can't work  this way but even the supreme court did  not apply that ruling retroactively so  again meaning people who had already  been convicted by a split jury remained  in prison what we're seeing now is a  campaign led by formerly incarcerated  people local activists public defenders  who are fighting to try to figure out  some level of recourse for the 1600 or  so people who are currently in prison in  louisiana having been convicted by a  divided jury and so in some cases  district attorneys are reviewing their  own cases and their jurisdictions as the  case in new orleans  there's discussion in the state  legislature about passing a new law that  maybe would grant automatic retrials to  people or have the governor's office  renew it but again in the meantime we've  got sixteen hundred people who were  convicted um in a way that the u.s  supreme court in a pretty scathing  opinion  led by conservative justices like neil  gorsuch that have said look this is  clearly racist clearly unconstitutional  and yet today as we're chatting anthony  bolt is still in angola one one person  would be too many 1600 is truly a  travesty we hope there is some movement  in their cases wesley lowry thank you  very much of course thanks for having me  you can watch wes's full report right  now on 60 minutes plus it's available on  the viacom cbs streaming app paramount  plus
 

